Z||000005||artherd||11-22-2001||10:57 PM||||Asside from price, and the ascendand build-quality, Fieros, Lambos, Lotui, and Ferraris share quite a bit (by necessity).<P>The mid-engine layout dictates much of what is done (gas tank in the middle, remote radiators up front, 2 seats etc.)<P>When GM designed the Fiero, they actually *purchased* several 308 Ferraris, to learn what Ferrari had learned during decades of designing road and race cars with the engine in back.  Nautrally, other cars with such an extreme performance layout make similar sacrafices, and design decisions.<P>Kinda cool to be in the company with the big boys, eh?<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SLYGUY:<BR><B>I couldn't believe how many similarities there are between the Fiero and this car. In every picture and at nearly every angle this car is a Fiero.<P>When you see a picture of the cabin you will agree. Deep buckets seperated by a tall center tunnel, similar dashboard, etc.<BR>Imitation is the sincerest form of flattery for sure!!!<P>Sly</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
